.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1tnjlu/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-.log:101:Architecture-decision brief from the active macOS-first goal. Treat TASK-260715-1tnjlu as the critical DNS policy entry point. Read .spec/architecture.md, .spec/security-privacy.md, .spec/relay-protocol.md, .spec/validation.md, .spec/decisions.md, the accepted M1 runtime/profile contracts, and relevant board dependencies. Compare explicit per-profile resolver endpoints, an accountable documented product default, exit-host discovery, and tunneled DoH on privacy, bootstrap circularity, IPv4/IPv6 reachability from the exit, UDP/TCP semantics, DNSSEC transparency, operator expectations, migration, M4 UI, M2 relay/degraded compatibility, testability, and operational cost. Preserve fail-closed DNS: after routes apply ordinary queries must never touch or silently fall back to the physical resolver; no remote shell parsing without a separately approved bounded security design; no fake DNS; no public resolver vendor selected by taste. Freeze exact schema fields, validation, address-family behavior, port, transport, timeout/failure semantics, migration and downstream task impacts only when derivable from accepted evidence. Numeric tuning remains injectable where the goal makes it an evidence gate. Cite concrete primary/project evidence and concrete downstream task IDs. An independent Codex reviewer may approve an architecture policy derived entirely from existing accepted invariants; if a genuinely accountable product choice remains that cannot be derived, attach a complete options/tradeoffs/recommendation packet, mark blocked, and request only the exact choice. Persist the decision in a task-scoped outcome and the project source of truth, run docs/spec/board consistency checks, and hand off to review.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1tnjlu/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-.log:2854:.spec/decisions.md:26:| ADR-022 | Proposed | Require an ordered, explicit per-profile `dnsResolver` containing one to four numeric IPv4/IPv6 DNS endpoints; select DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH for M1 and the same endpoints for M2 UDP with TCP fallback. There is no Relux/product resolver default, exit-host discovery, hostname resolver endpoint, tunneled DoH, or physical-resolver fallback in the baseline. Exact schema, validation, timeout, failure, and migration semantics are recorded by TASK-260715-1tnjlu; independent architecture review is the approval boundary. |
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1tnjlu/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-.log:3021:.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260721-2raag7_add-explicit-exit-resolver-profile-experience/README.md:7:In scope: shared presentation state and actions; ordered one-to-four endpoint editing; add, remove, and reorder; IPv4 and IPv6 literal plus port entry; port 53 presentation default; field-level validation; requiresResolverConfiguration remediation; repository publication and revision conflicts; active-profile safeguards; iOS and macOS SwiftUI surfaces; accessibility identifiers; snapshots; Page Object UI tests. Out of scope: changing TASK-260715-1tnjlu policy, selecting or prefilling a public resolver, hostname endpoints, DoH, exit-host discovery, DNS transport implementation, final marketing copy, and physical leak validation.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-1tnjlu/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_spawn-log_-analyst--solution-architect--codex-.log:3022:.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-tx1tbz_profile-and-key-management/TASK-260721-2raag7_add-explicit-exit-resolver-profile-experience/README.md:10:1. The shared editor and both Apple surfaces present one to four ordered numeric resolver endpoints with address and port controls, default an omitted port to 53, and never prefill a product, physical, public-vendor, loopback, or discovered resolver address. 2. Validation and normalized save values exactly match TASK-260721-33o8fc, including deliberate loopback/private/ULA support and field-specific errors for hostnames, zones, invalid families, duplicates, invalid counts, and ports. 3. A legacy profile without dnsResolver remains visible with requiresResolverConfiguration remediation, cannot be selected for a new connection until valid input is saved, and is never silently upgraded from system DNS. 4. Add, remove, reorder, save, discard, relaunch, concurrent revision conflict, selected-profile change, and active-profile edit paths produce deterministic repository generations and accessible recovery on iOS and macOS. 5. Swift tests, snapshots, and iOS/macOS Page Object journeys cover valid IPv4-only, IPv6-only, dual ordered, loopback, invalid, migration, and conflict states with stable identifiers, keyboard and VoiceOver behavior, screenshot inspection, and no DNS network dependency.
